Myopia progression
Young Adult & Adolecence
Blurred distance vision, Frequent squinting, Normal near vision, Myopic shift on refraction

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-030
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe, daily frontal tension headaches, profound eye strain, and psychological distress severely impacting academic performance.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an adolescent experiencing severe, daily, debilitating frontal headaches and visual fatigue resulting in acute school refusal and extreme distress during exam preparation. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was necessary to rule out acute intracranial pathology (e.g., space-occupying lesion) causing the headaches, and to perform a basic neurological and visual assessment to provide immediate clinical clarity and pain management prior to routine clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head pounds constantly across my forehead every time I try to look at the board or study, and I have to squint so hard just to see anything far away."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 6-month history of progressive blurring of distance vision, which has recently culminated in severe, daily frontal tension headaches following prolonged visual tasks. The parent notes the patient has a frequent need to sit closer to the television and the front of the classroom. The patient admits to frequent squinting in an attempt to improve uncorrected visual acuity, which directly correlates with the onset of the frontal tension headaches. There is frequent eye rubbing secondary to profound eye strain and excessive blinking. The patient reports being entirely unaware of distant objects but notes completely normal visual acuity when reading or performing near-vision tasks. The acute distress tonight was precipitated by an inability to complete exam studying due to the severity of the headache and visual fatigue.
Functional Status: Currently unable to read or look at screens without precipitating a severe headache; high academic distress.
Social/Safety Concerns: Significant impact on education and mental well-being; high anxiety regarding the daily pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 55 Kg
General: Patient appears fatigued, frequently rubbing the eyes, and rubbing the bilateral frontal/temporal regions.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive neurological examination is completely normal. Cranial nerves II-XII are intact. Fundoscopy reveals sharp optic discs with no evidence of papilloedema, ruling out raised intracranial pressure. Extraocular movements are full and smooth. Confrontational visual fields are full. Gross visual acuity testing (using a makeshift 3-meter distance target) reveals significantly impaired distance vision, while near vision (reading a mobile phone at 30cm) is perfectly intact. The presentation strongly aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of blurred distance vision, frequent squinting, normal near vision, and strongly anticipates a myopic shift on formal cycloplegic objective refraction testing. Palpation of the pericranial musculature reveals tightness in the frontalis and temporalis muscles.
Environmental Observations: Patient's study desk has poor ambient lighting; a small tablet screen is positioned excessively close to the face.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications. Patient has taken Paracetamol with minimal relief for the eye-strain headaches.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Uncorrected Myopia Progression causing Severe Tension Headaches - Status: Symptomatic Exacerbation
Differentials:

Space-Occupying Lesion / Raised ICP - Ruled out/unlikely due to the normal fundoscopy (no papilloedema), the lack of neurological focal deficits, and the clear correlation of the headache exclusively with visual tasks and squinting.

Migraine - Ruled out/unlikely due to the bilateral, non-throbbing, frontal band-like nature of the pain, the lack of nausea/vomiting, and the explicit trigger of prolonged distance-vision straining.

Convergence Insufficiency - Ruled out/unlikely due to the completely normal near vision without diplopia, whereas the primary deficit is exclusively isolated to distance vision.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (High academic/psychological impact)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with severe, debilitating daily headaches and acute academic distress, prompting fear of a serious neurological issue. The immediate after-hours home visit was required to physically rule out signs of raised intracranial pressure and focal neurological deficits. The clinical examination safely excluded acute intracranial pathology and confirmed that the frontal headaches are secondary to chronic, severe frontalis muscle tension caused by constant squinting to compensate for uncorrected myopia. Delaying assessment would have prolonged the patient's severe anxiety and pain. The visit provided a definitive, benign mechanical explanation for the headaches, instantly de-escalating the crisis and redirecting the care pathway to optometry.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided direct clinical reassurance that the headaches are muscular (tension) in origin, directly caused by eye strain, and not a brain tumor. Advised the use of over-the-counter Ibuprofen for acute relief of the muscular headache. Generated an urgent referral for a comprehensive optometric review to perform formal cycloplegic objective refraction testing and prescribe corrective lenses.
Positive Behavioral Change: To immediately reduce the muscular strain causing your headaches, implement the 20-20-20 rule during study: every 20 minutes, look at an object 20 feet away for exactly 20 seconds, actively forcing the ciliary muscles inside your eyes to relax. To physically prevent the need to squint, move your desk closer to the window and use a bright desk lamp, acknowledging that adequate light dramatically reduces the focusing effort required by the eye.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent Optometry review within 7 days for corrective lenses; GP review only required if headaches persist after wearing the new glasses.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the headache suddenly becomes explosive, if the patient develops vomiting without nausea, or if they experience sudden, painless loss of vision in one eye.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The neurological exam is normal, indicating the headaches are from eye strain; definitive vision correction requires an optometrist.
Clinical Course: The tension headaches will almost entirely resolve once the correct glasses or contact lenses are prescribed and the physical need to squint is eliminated.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if your headache suddenly changes to a severe, explosive pain, if you begin vomiting, or if you lose vision entirely in one or both eyes.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your eyes have physically elongated slightly (myopia), making distant objects blurry. Because you cannot see the board, you are subconsciously squinting constantly. This squinting severely overworks the muscles in your forehead, leading to the painful tension headaches you experience every afternoon.
Actionable Steps: Stop trying to force yourself to read the board from the back of the room; move to the front immediately to stop the squinting until you get glasses. Take Ibuprofen for the forehead pain tonight, and prioritize getting an eye exam this week.

